    "avalanche_comments": null,
    "snowpack_summary": "<p>The height of snow doubled with over 1 meter of settled storm snow from March 8-13th. The hardness profile is generally right-side-up, progressing from softer to harder. Boot Penetration is 50-80cm with ski penetration ~40cm. Trailbreaking is still relatively deep. We saw significant signs of settlement, including large settlement cones with cracks and wrinkles near depressions and features.</p>\n<p>Where observed, layers in the upper snowpack are well-bonded, including to both the 3/12 crust, &darr;60-80cm, and the 3/8 crust, &darr;110-120cm. A slight weak layer exists above the 3/8 crust, but it's generally not problematic, and didn't produce test results. The old mid-Feb cruts/weak layers, &darr;130-160cm, have transformed into melt forms and are mostly unrecognizable. Of note, for the upcoming rain/wetting, there's a relatively thick, weak layer of moist, 4Finger-4F+ hard melt forms about 150cm below the surface.</p>\n<p>See profile graph from E, 5380ft.</p>",
    "snowpack": null,
